<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- App Transport Security.

	     Apple blocks plain http by default. That is the right default for the
	     app's own traffic and it stays in force there: the Gemini / OpenAI /
	     Anthropic calls, GitHub, Google Drive and Calendar all still have to be
	     https, because this exception does not cover them.

	     University portals are a different world. Plenty of college ERPs in
	     India are still http-only, or start on https and redirect to http, and
	     a portal that can't load is a portal you can't use. Since the app now
	     lets you add whichever portal your college runs, the web view has to be
	     allowed to load them.

	     NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsInWebContent is the narrow key for exactly this:
	     it relaxes the rule for WKWebView content only, not for URLSession.
	     Nothing the app itself sends leaves the device unencrypted.

	     Note for the App Store: this key needs a justification at review.
	     "The app embeds university portals chosen by the user, many of which
	     are http-only" is the reason. -->
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoadsInWebContent</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>
	<!-- Files you pick, drop or save — macOS 15 and later.

	     LifeTracker is not sandboxed; it runs under the Hardened Runtime. On
	     recent macOS that is no longer enough on its own: reading anything in
	     Desktop, Documents, Downloads, an external disk or a network share is
	     gated by TCC, and an app with no usage string for that location is
	     refused *without ever showing a prompt*.

	     That is what broke pushing to GitHub after a macOS upgrade: files
	     dragged in from Downloads could not be read, so a push went up with
	     nothing in it, while Colab notebooks — which the app downloads into
	     its own temporary folder — kept working. With these keys present
	     macOS asks once, per folder, and remembers the answer. -->
	<key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>LifeTracker needs to read the files you drop in or pick from your Desktop so it can push them to GitHub and file them into a subject.</string>
	<key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>LifeTracker needs to read the files you drop in or pick from Documents so it can push them to GitHub and file them into a subject.</string>
	<key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>LifeTracker needs to read the files you drop in or pick from Downloads so it can push them to GitHub and file them into a subject.</string>
	<key>NSRemovableVolumesUsageDescription</key>
	<string>LifeTracker needs to read files you drop in from a USB drive or external disk so it can push them to GitHub.</string>
	<key>NSNetworkVolumesUsageDescription</key>
	<string>LifeTracker needs to read files you drop in from a network share so it can push them to GitHub.</string>
	<key>NSFileProviderDomainUsageDescription</key>
	<string>LifeTracker needs to read files you drop in from iCloud Drive, Google Drive or Dropbox so it can push them to GitHub.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
